DEV9: implement ATA PIO data-out, so the HDD can be written to.

PR SCOPE: OUT. A DEV9/ATA capability gap, not TLB/MMU/syscall. Needed for a
writable HDD, not to reach a bash prompt. Worth proposing separately, on its
own merits and with its own testing.

PCSX2 implements every PIO read command but no PIO write command, and has no
host-to-device DRQ path at all: DRQCmdPIODataToHost() exists, its opposite does
not, and ATA::Write() has no case for ATA_R_DATA. Commands 0x30 WRITE SECTOR(S),
0x34, 0xC5 WRITE MULTIPLE and 0x39 all fall through to HDD_Unk().

Games never notice, because Sony's atad driver uses DMA (0xC8/0xCA, both already
implemented). Anything driving the drive by PIO -- which is what Linux does when
IDE DMA is off -- gets no DRQ after issuing the command, and every write fails:

    hda: status error: ... DriveReady SeekComplete Error
    hda: drive status error ... no DRQ after issuing WRITE

Real drives accept PIO writes; this is emulation the hardware has and PCSX2
lacks. DMA is not a substitute on PS2 Linux, whose DMA path calls out over SIF
RPC to an IOP-side server that ships in Sony's RTE rather than driving ATA from
the EE at all.

Implementation mirrors the existing read path:

  DRQCmdPIODataFromHost()  clears BUSY, asserts DRQ, arms one 256-word sector.
                           The first block of a data-out command raises no
                           INTRQ -- the host polls for DRQ, and INTRQ follows
                           only blocks the device has already taken.
  PostCmdPIODataFromHost() moves the completed sector into currentWrite, then
                           either arms the next block (interrupting on
                           sectorsPerInterrupt boundaries, as
                           HDD_ReadPIOEndBlock does) or enqueues a
                           WriteQueueEntry and honours fetWriteCacheEnabled.
  ATAwritePIO()            the ATA_R_DATA sink.

ATA::Write() needed one further change: its guard dropped every write while
BUSY|DRQ was set, and PIO data-out writes the data register precisely while DRQ
is asserted, so it would have swallowed every byte. ATA_R_DATA is now refused
only on BUSY.

Buffer handling and the write queue are the DMA path's, unchanged, so both write
modes converge on the same disk plumbing.

Tested: PS2 Linux 2.4.17 on an ext2 filesystem served as the HDD image --
mounted read-write, unpacked a 175-file source tree, ran e2fsck clean afterwards
on the host, and booted a full Debian userspace from it. NOT tested against any
game; PIO write cannot be reached by one, since games use DMA.

